    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="Inscr.">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Hu">Hu</ref> Inscr.</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="Hu Inscr." corresp="#wit-b">Þis is seo gerædnyss, hu mon þæt hundred haldan sceal.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hu Inscr." corresp="#wit-q">Hoc est iudicium, qualiter hundredum teneri debeat.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hu Inscr." corresp="#wit-cons-cn-2">Hec est consiliatio, quomodo centenarii conuentus obseruari debet.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">Ðis is seo gerædnyss hu mon þæt hundred haldan sceal.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">This is the ordinance concerning the administration of the hundred.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Dies ist die Verordnung, wie das Hundert[gericht] gehalten werden soll.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">This is the ordinance on how the hundred[-court] is to be held.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">K is the Quadripartitus Latin (as in the sheet's layout); Liebermann's base Old English text is B (CCCC 383 f. 1, c. 1125); the Consiliatio Cnuti, Appendix, is in AF. Liebermann dates it 946–c. 961 and tentatively assigns it to Edgar (‘I Eadgar?’). B's heading is in black in the same line as the text; it stood in the archetype.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
